    "author_association": "COLLABORATOR",
    "body": "### Problem\n\n## Why this matters\n\n- Shared context would let a new worker inherit project facts, recent decisions, rejected approaches, and task boundaries without repeating discovery.\n- In team or Cloud settings, several humans and several workers may act in parallel on the same repository, so useful context should survive beyond one chat transcript.\n- The orchestrator will make better decisions if it knows what has already been tried, what is blocked, which product decisions are still active, and which codebase areas are already owned.\n\n\n### Proposed direction\n\n## Questions to answer\n\n- What should be session-local, worker-shared, project-shared, and user-shared?\n- What information is safe and useful to persist versus too noisy or too risky to store?\n- How should humans inspect, correct, and delete memory when the system is wrong?\n\n## Outcome\n\nDefine the smallest shared context model that improves both worker quality and orchestrator decision-making.\n\n\n### Alternatives considered\n\n_No response_\n\n### Before submitting\n\n- [x] I searched existing issues / discussions for something similar\n- [ ] I am willing to help implement this if maintainers agree on scope",
